WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.205
A maintenance service exposes GET /tickets/731.

00:00:03.205 --> 00:00:09.136
A successful response includes a ticket identifier,
a status and an estimated duration.

00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:15.409
The JSON shape alone is insufficient: clients need
to know whether duration means minutes or

00:00:15.409 --> 00:00:21.477
seconds, whether a missing estimate is null or
omitted, and what happens when a ticket is

00:00:21.477 --> 00:00:22.295
unavailable.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:28.231
An API contract therefore includes meaning and
failure behavior as well as a syntactically valid

00:00:28.231 --> 00:00:28.725
example.

00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:31.938
Choose the method before inventing an endpoint name.

00:00:31.938 --> 00:00:35.150
A read should not close the ticket as a side effect.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:41.143
A replacement request to a known ticket target
differs from submitting a new work request for the

00:00:41.143 --> 00:00:42.317
service to process.

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:48.253
Document authorization failures and absent resources
so clients can react appropriately.

00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:54.796
Use HTTPS for transport protection, and avoid
placing access tokens in example query strings that

00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:56.213
might appear in logs.

00:00:56.213 --> 00:01:02.419
These decisions help separate an interoperable
service boundary from a collection of loosely

00:01:02.419 --> 00:01:04.038
specified network calls.

00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:09.707
A client submits a repair request and loses its
connection before receiving the response.

00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:13.082
It cannot tell whether the server committed the
work.

00:01:13.082 --> 00:01:18.560
Retrying is therefore an uncertainty problem, not
proof that the first attempt failed.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.547
A stable target helps: repeating a request to set
ticket 731 to the same intended state can be

00:01:24.547 --> 00:01:28.177
designed to have the same intended effect as one
attempt.

00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:32.437
That does not require identical response bytes or
status codes.

00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:37.913
Contrast this with an operation that closes
whichever ticket is currently oldest.

00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:42.240
After the first success, a retry could close a
different ticket.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.041
The resource identity moves, so repetition changes
the intended effect.

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:50.624
Use an explicit ticket identifier for that
operation.

00:01:50.624 --> 00:01:56.777
For creation through POST, define a deduplication
mechanism if retries must not create another

00:01:56.777 --> 00:01:57.301
request.

00:01:57.301 --> 00:02:03.323
PUT and DELETE have idempotent method semantics;
POST and PATCH are not generally guaranteed

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.043
idempotent.

00:02:04.043 --> 00:02:10.458
The implementation must honor the chosen contract
rather than relying on the method name to repair

00:02:10.458 --> 00:02:11.506
unsafe behavior.
